<Window xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="UI Standard Showcase"
        Height="560" Width="820" MinHeight="430" MinWidth="660"
        WindowStyle="None"
        ResizeMode="CanResizeWithGrip"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
        Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeDialogBg}"
        Foreground="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"
        FontFamily="Segoe UI"
        FontSize="12"
        UseLayoutRounding="True"
        SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
        TextOptions.TextFormattingMode="Display">

    <!--
    ===================================================================================
    T3LAB MASTER REFERENCE UI — REVIT DIALOG
    ===================================================================================
    Not a styled window: the anatomy of a real Revit dialog, read out of
    UIFramework.dll (UIFramework.PdfImport.PdfImportDialog, Revit 2026) and
    reproduced. See docs/revit-native-ui.md for how that was obtained.

        Autodesk.UI.Windows.ChildWindow
        └ DockPanel                       ChildWindow_Panel_Margin
          ├ MainTop     (Dock=Top)        label + field rows
          ├ MainBottom  (Dock=Bottom)     help link left · OK / Cancel right
          └ content                       fills the rest

    Three rules make it read as Revit rather than as a redesign:

    1. WE DRAW THE CAPTION, AND IT KEEPS MIN / MAX / CLOSE.
       Revit's own dialogs use the native Windows title bar — ChildWindow does
       not re-template Window, it only adjusts Win32 styles (WS_CAPTION,
       WS_SYSMENU, WS_EX_DLGMODALFRAME, WS_EX_CONTEXTHELP).

       We cannot follow it there, because a native caption is painted from the
       WINDOWS theme: Revit-dark on a light Windows renders a white title bar
       above a dark window. Re-tinting it needs DwmSetWindowAttribute, a
       P/Invoke IronPython cannot declare. So this window owns its caption and
       paints it from the same T3Theme* tokens as the body — the only pure-WPF
       way to make it follow the host.

       All THREE buttons stay (min / max / close). A Revit modal dialog shows
       only a close box; a T3Lab tool window is resizable and long-running, so
       this is a deliberate difference. Do not "correct" it. See
       docs/revit-native-ui.md.

    2. NO ORNAMENT OF OUR OWN. No drop shadows, no gradients, no filled accent
       buttons, no icon toolbar, no pill badges. Revit dialogs have none of
       these, and each costs a render pass we do not need to spend.

       CORNER RADIUS — four values, each borrowed, none invented:
           8   the WINDOW FRAME only. Windows 11 rounds every top-level window
               at 8px, Revit's dialogs included; WindowChrome.CornerRadius and
               the outer Border both carry it so the content clips to the frame.
           0   surfaces, fields, group boxes, lists, popups, the progress
               groove. Inside the frame, a Revit dialog is square.
           4   anything clickable: push buttons, caption buttons, rail tiles.
               This is not our number — it is what Windows 11 draws, checked
               against Revit's own OK/Cancel/Apply/Help row.
           2   the check box, because a 13px control cannot carry 4.
       The scroll thumb is the one exception and is rounded by nature.
       If you are about to add a fourth value, you are designing, not matching.

    3. STOCK CONTROL SHAPES. The templates below exist only because Revit's dark
       theme needs them — a default WPF control stays light on a dark host. They
       reproduce the platform control; they do not reinvent it.

    HOW REVIT ACTUALLY SEPARATES A DIALOG. Checked against Apply View Template,
    which is the ground truth for this:

        caption      WHITE, LIGHTER than the body — not a darker chrome band.
                     ChromeBackgroundColor (#D9D9D9) belongs to the main window
                     and is wrong in a dialog.
        body         one light grey, and that is the whole background
        group box    NO fill. A thin border with the label sitting on it.
        lists/fields WHITE, against the grey body — this is where nearly all of
                     the separation comes from
        footer       no strip of its own, just a rule above the button row
        selection    a SOLID accent row with white text, never a grey tint

    So the structure is carried by white surfaces and hairlines, not by a stack
    of greys. Two mistakes were made here before that was checked: a #D9D9D9
    caption band and a tinted footer strip, both invented.

    Colour is not chosen here either: every brush is {DynamicResource T3Theme*}
    from GUI/RevitTheme.py, which reads the brushes Revit is painting itself
    with. The only literal hex in the file is the copyright amber, fixed by the
    T3Lab standard.
    ===================================================================================
    -->

    <WindowChrome.WindowChrome>
        <WindowChrome CaptionHeight="56"
                      ResizeBorderThickness="6"
                      GlassFrameThickness="0"
                      CornerRadius="8"
                      UseAeroCaptionButtons="False"/>
    </WindowChrome.WindowChrome>

    <Window.Resources>

        <!-- Fallbacks. RevitTheme.apply() overwrites every one at load; these
             exist so the file still renders standalone in a designer. -->
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeDialogBg"              Color="#F5F5F5"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeChromeBg"               Color="#FFFFFF"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeStatusBarBg"            Color="#F5F5F5"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeChatBg"                Color="#FFFFFF"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeCardBg"                Color="#FFFFFF"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeCardBorder"            Color="#DEDEDE"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemePaneEdge"              Color="#DEDEDE"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeDivider"               Color="#ECECEC"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeInk"                   Color="#3C3C3C"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeMuted"                 Color="#5A5A5A"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeFaint"                 Color="#ABABAB"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeAccent"                Color="#0696D7"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeOnAccent"              Color="#FFFFFF"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeCheckFill"             Color="#0696D7"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeControlBgAlt"          Color="#F5F5F5"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeSuccess"               Color="#0B7A4A"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeDanger"                Color="#C42B1C"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeWarning"               Color="#9A6700"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeIconFg"                Color="#919191"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeScrollThumb"           Color="#C1C1C1"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeGroupBorder"           Color="#E5E5E5"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeGroupHeaderFg"         Color="#3C3C3C"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeControlBg"             Color="#FFFFFF"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeControlBorder"         Color="#CCCCCC"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeControlHoverBg"        Color="#F0F0F0"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeControlHoverBorder"    Color="#B7B7B7"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeControlPressedBg"      Color="#E6E6E6"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeControlPressedBorder"  Color="#AFAFAF"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeControlDisabledBg"     Color="#F5F5F5"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeControlDisabledBorder" Color="#E5E5E5"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeControlDisabledFg"     Color="#A7A7A7"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeFieldBg"               Color="#FFFFFF"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeFieldBorder"           Color="#DEDEDE"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeFieldFocusBorder"      Color="#0696D7"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeHeaderBg"              Color="#F0F0F0"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeHeaderFg"              Color="#3C3C3C"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeRowAltBg"              Color="#FAFAFA"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeRowHoverBg"            Color="#F3F3F3"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeRowSelectedBg"         Color="#0696D7"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeGridLine"              Color="#ECECEC"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeTrackBg"               Color="#ECECEC"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeProgressTrack"         Color="#CECECE"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeProgressBorder"        Color="#B7B7B7"/>
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="T3ThemeProgressFill"          Color="#38ABDF"/>

        <!-- ═══ TYPE ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             One size, one weight. Revit labels everything at the body size and
             lets colour carry the hierarchy; a dialog with four type sizes stops
             reading as part of the host.
             ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="FieldLabel" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"     Value="0,0,0,3"/>
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="MutedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="Foreground"        Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeMuted}"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ PUSH BUTTON ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
             75 x 23, 1px border, 4px radius — the shape Windows 11 gives a
             dialog button, matched against Revit's own footer row. The default
             action is marked by its BORDER; a filled accent button is the
             loudest "this was redesigned" signal a host dialog can carry, and
             Revit's own OK button is an outline.
             ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="DialogButton" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Background"          Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlBg}"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground"          Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"         Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlBorder}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness"     Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="MinWidth"            Value="75"/>
            <Setter Property="Height"              Value="23"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"             Value="10,0"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"              Value="4,0,0,0"/>
            <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="bd"
                                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                                CornerRadius="4"
                                SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                              Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background"  Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlHoverBg}"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlHoverBorder}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background"  Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlPressedBg}"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlPressedBorder}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlHoverBorder}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background"  Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledBg}"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledBorder}"/>
                                <Setter Property="Foreground"                  Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledFg}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- DEFAULT BUTTON. The accent ring is 1px, the same hairline every other
             button carries — only the colour changes. At 2px it reads as a heavy
             blue frame and pulls the whole footer toward it; the marker only has
             to be legible, not loud. -->
        <Style x:Key="DialogDefaultButton" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource DialogButton}">
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"     Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeAccent}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="IsDefault"       Value="True"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- Narrower button for the strip under a list — the Manage Links idiom. -->
        <Style x:Key="ListActionButton" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource DialogButton}">
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="64"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"   Value="0,0,6,0"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ TEXT BOX ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="DialogTextBox" TargetType="TextBox">
            <Setter Property="Background"               Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBg}"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground"               Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
            <Setter Property="CaretBrush"               Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"              Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBorder}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness"          Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="Height"                   Value="22"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"                  Value="4,0"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
            <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels"      Value="True"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="TextBox">
                        <Border x:Name="bd"
                                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                                SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                            <ScrollViewer x:Name="PART_ContentHost"
                                          Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                          VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlHoverBorder}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocusWithin" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldFocusBorder}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledBg}"/>
                                <Setter Property="Foreground"                 Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledFg}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ COMBO BOX — field plus a 17px arrow well, Revit's type selector.
             Flat popup: no shadow, no radius. ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="DialogComboBoxItem" TargetType="ComboBoxItem">
            <Setter Property="Padding"    Value="6,3"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="ComboBoxItem">
                        <Border x:Name="bd" Background="Transparent" Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                            <ContentPresenter VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsHighlighted" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeRowSelectedBg}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="DialogComboBox" TargetType="ComboBox">
            <Setter Property="Background"          Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBg}"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground"          Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"         Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBorder}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness"     Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="Height"              Value="22"/>
            <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True"/>
            <Setter Property="ItemContainerStyle"  Value="{StaticResource DialogComboBoxItem}"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="ComboBox">
                        <Grid>
                            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="17"/>
                            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                            <Border x:Name="bd" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                                    Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                    BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                    BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                                    SnapsToDevicePixels="True"/>

                            <ToggleButton Grid.ColumnSpan="2" Focusable="False" ClickMode="Press"
                                          IsChecked="{Binding IsDropDownOpen, Mode=TwoWay, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}">
                                <ToggleButton.Template>
                                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="ToggleButton">
                                        <Border Background="Transparent"/>
                                    </ControlTemplate>
                                </ToggleButton.Template>
                            </ToggleButton>

                            <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="0"
                                              Content="{TemplateBinding SelectionBoxItem}"
                                              Margin="5,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                              IsHitTestVisible="False"/>

                            <!-- A Path, not a glyph font: no font lookup, no fallback
                                 scan, and it inherits the theme brush directly. -->
                            <Path Grid.Column="1" Data="M 0 0 L 7 0 L 3.5 4 Z"
                                  Fill="{DynamicResource T3ThemeIconFg}"
                                  HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                  IsHitTestVisible="False"/>

                            <Popup x:Name="PART_Popup" Placement="Bottom"
                                   IsOpen="{TemplateBinding IsDropDownOpen}"
                                   AllowsTransparency="True" Focusable="False" PopupAnimation="None">
                                <Border Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeCardBg}"
                                        BorderBrush="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlBorder}"
                                        BorderThickness="1"
                                        MinWidth="{TemplateBinding ActualWidth}"
                                        MaxHeight="{TemplateBinding MaxDropDownHeight}"
                                        SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                                    <ScrollViewer>
                                        <StackPanel IsItemsHost="True" KeyboardNavigation.DirectionalNavigation="Contained"/>
                                    </ScrollViewer>
                                </Border>
                            </Popup>
                        </Grid>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlHoverBorder}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsDropDownOpen" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldFocusBorder}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledBg}"/>
                                <Setter Property="Foreground"                 Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledFg}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ SEARCH FIELD ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
             Revit does not label its search box. The magnifier sits inside the
             field on the left and the prompt text carries the meaning, so the
             row reads as one control instead of "label + box". Icon is a Path,
             not a glyph font — no font resolution, and it takes the theme brush.
             ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="SearchBoxChrome" TargetType="Border">
            <Setter Property="Background"          Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBg}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"         Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBorder}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness"     Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius"        Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Height"              Value="22"/>
            <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ CHECK BOX ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             13 x 13, 2px radius. CHECKED IS AN ACCENT-FILLED BOX WITH A WHITE
             TICK — not a white box with a coloured tick.

             Size is the classic Win32 metric, which is what a Revit dialog
             shows next to 12px label text. 16px is the Windows 11 size and
             reads oversized here: against a 12px label it draws the eye to the
             box instead of the option. Checked in a render before landing.
             ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="DialogCheckBox" TargetType="CheckBox">
            <Setter Property="Foreground"          Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"              Value="0,0,0,6"/>
            <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="CheckBox">
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Background="Transparent">
                            <Border x:Name="box" Width="13" Height="13" CornerRadius="2"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBg}"
                                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBorder}"
                                    BorderThickness="1"
                                    VerticalAlignment="Center" SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                                <!-- The checked state is an IMAGE, because that is what
                                     Revit uses: CheckBoxCheckedImage_LightTheme /
                                     _DarkTheme in its own dictionary. RevitTheme.apply()
                                     publishes the host image under this key, or a drawn
                                     stand-in with the same geometry when the host has
                                     none. Binding the source means the tick is Revit's
                                     own artwork rather than a colour we matched by eye. -->
                                <Image x:Name="tick" Stretch="Uniform" Margin="-1"
                                       Source="{DynamicResource T3ThemeCheckedGlyph}"
                                       Visibility="Collapsed"
                                       RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode="HighQuality"/>
                            </Border>
                            <ContentPresenter Margin="7,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </StackPanel>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <!-- Background stays transparent under the glyph: the host
                                 image already carries the filled square. Only the drawn
                                 fallback needs the fill, and it carries its own. -->
                            <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="box"  Property="Background"     Value="Transparent"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="box"  Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="tick" Property="Visibility"      Value="Visible"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="box" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlHoverBorder}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <MultiTrigger>
                                <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                                    <Condition Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True"/>
                                    <Condition Property="IsChecked"   Value="False"/>
                                </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                                <Setter TargetName="box" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlHoverBg}"/>
                            </MultiTrigger>
                            <MultiTrigger>
                                <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                                    <Condition Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True"/>
                                    <Condition Property="IsChecked"   Value="True"/>
                                </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                                <Setter TargetName="tick" Property="Opacity" Value="0.85"/>
                            </MultiTrigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                                <Setter TargetName="box" Property="Background"  Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledBg}"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="box" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledBorder}"/>
                                <Setter Property="Foreground"                   Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledFg}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="DialogRadioButton" TargetType="RadioButton">
            <Setter Property="Foreground"          Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"              Value="0,0,0,6"/>
            <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="RadioButton">
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Background="Transparent">
                            <Grid Width="13" Height="13" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                <Ellipse x:Name="ring"
                                         Fill="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBg}"
                                         Stroke="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBorder}" StrokeThickness="1"/>
                                <Ellipse x:Name="dot" Width="5" Height="5"
                                         Fill="{DynamicResource T3ThemeOnAccent}" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
                            </Grid>
                            <ContentPresenter Margin="7,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </StackPanel>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <!-- Accent-filled ring with a white centre, the same
                                 language as the check box above. Inferred from that
                                 evidence, not separately verified against Revit. -->
                            <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="ring" Property="Fill"       Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeAccent}"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="ring" Property="Stroke"     Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeAccent}"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="dot"  Property="Visibility" Value="Visible"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="ring" Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlHoverBorder}"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="ring" Property="Fill"   Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlHoverBg}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                                <Setter TargetName="ring" Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledBorder}"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="ring" Property="Fill"   Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledBg}"/>
                                <Setter Property="Foreground"               Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeControlDisabledFg}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ GROUP BOX — Revit's section frame: a hairline rectangle with the
             caption notched into the top-left of the border. No fill, no radius,
             no card. This is the container a tool reaches for first, and the one
             piece of structure that carries the composition.
             ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="DialogGroupBox" TargetType="GroupBox">
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"         Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeGroupBorder}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness"     Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"             Value="9,7"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"              Value="0,0,0,11"/>
            <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="GroupBox">
                        <Grid>
                            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                                <RowDefinition Height="8"/>
                                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                                <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
                            </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                            <Border Grid.Row="1" Grid.RowSpan="2" Background="Transparent"
                                    BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                    BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                                    SnapsToDevicePixels="True"/>
                            <Border Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="2"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeDialogBg}"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                                    Margin="8,0,0,0" Padding="4,0">
                                <ContentPresenter ContentSource="Header"
                                                  TextBlock.Foreground="{DynamicResource T3ThemeGroupHeaderFg}"/>
                            </Border>
                            <ContentPresenter Grid.Row="2" Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"/>
                        </Grid>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ PROGRESS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             HigProgressCtrlFront (#38ABDF) over HigProgressCtrlBkg, flat, with
             a 1px groove. Read out of Revit's own ThemeColor dictionary, so it
             is the bar a Revit DIALOG shows. The green Aero gradient is the
             Win32 control in the main window's status bar — a different thing,
             and wrong in here.
             ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="DialogProgressBar" TargetType="ProgressBar">
            <Setter Property="Height"          Value="12"/>
            <Setter Property="Background"      Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeProgressTrack}"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground"      Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeProgressFill}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"     Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeProgressBorder}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="ProgressBar">
                        <Border Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                                SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                            <Grid Margin="1">
                                <Rectangle x:Name="PART_Track"/>
                                <Rectangle x:Name="PART_Indicator"
                                           Fill="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                                           RadiusX="1" RadiusY="1"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Left"/>
                            </Grid>
                        </Border>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ SCROLLBAR — ultra-thin, thumb only. T3Lab standard on both
             tracks, and the one place a rounded corner earns its keep. ═══════ -->
        <Style x:Key="ThinScrollBar" TargetType="ScrollBar">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="Width"      Value="8"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="ScrollBar">
                        <Grid Background="Transparent">
                            <Track x:Name="PART_Track" IsDirectionReversed="True">
                                <Track.DecreaseRepeatButton>
                                    <RepeatButton Command="ScrollBar.PageUpCommand" Opacity="0" Focusable="False"/>
                                </Track.DecreaseRepeatButton>
                                <Track.Thumb>
                                    <Thumb>
                                        <Thumb.Template>
                                            <ControlTemplate TargetType="Thumb">
                                                <Border Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeScrollThumb}"
                                                        CornerRadius="4" Margin="2,0"/>
                                            </ControlTemplate>
                                        </Thumb.Template>
                                    </Thumb>
                                </Track.Thumb>
                                <Track.IncreaseRepeatButton>
                                    <RepeatButton Command="ScrollBar.PageDownCommand" Opacity="0" Focusable="False"/>
                                </Track.IncreaseRepeatButton>
                            </Track>
                        </Grid>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="Orientation" Value="Horizontal">
                                <Setter Property="Height" Value="8"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="PART_Track" Property="IsDirectionReversed" Value="False"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ GRID — Revit's schedule metrics: 22px rows, hairline rules, grey
             header with vertical dividers, blue-tint selection. ═══════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="GridHeader" TargetType="DataGridColumnHeader">
            <Setter Property="Background"                 Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeHeaderBg}"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground"                 Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeHeaderFg}"/>
            <Setter Property="FontWeight"                 Value="Normal"/>
            <Setter Property="Height"                     Value="23"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"                    Value="6,0"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"                Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeCardBorder}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness"            Value="0,0,1,1"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment"   Value="Center"/>
            <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels"        Value="True"/>
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="GridRow" TargetType="DataGridRow">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFieldBg}"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
            <Setter Property="Height"     Value="22"/>
            <Style.Triggers>
                <Trigger Property="ItemsControl.AlternationIndex" Value="1">
                    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeRowAltBg}"/>
                </Trigger>
                <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeRowHoverBg}"/>
                </Trigger>
                <!-- Accent fill with white text. Revit's own list selection is a
                     solid blue row, not a grey tint - see the Apply View Template
                     dialog. -->
                <Trigger Property="IsSelected" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeRowSelectedBg}"/>
                    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeOnAccent}"/>
                </Trigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>

        <!-- The cell must be transparent and untemplated, otherwise WPF's default
             cell chrome repaints the selection in the system highlight blue and
             none of the row tokens above ever show. -->
        <Style x:Key="GridCell" TargetType="DataGridCell">
            <Setter Property="Background"       Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness"  Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"          Value="6,0"/>
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{x:Null}"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="DataGridCell">
                        <Border Background="Transparent" Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                            <ContentPresenter VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>


        <!-- ═══ WINDOW CHROME ═════════════════════════════════════════════════
             We draw the caption. Not because a custom caption is better — a
             Revit dialog uses the native one — but because the native one obeys
             the WINDOWS theme, so on Revit-dark + Windows-light it renders a
             white title bar on a dark window. Owning it is the only way in pure
             WPF to make the caption follow Revit. Everything it paints comes
             from the same T3Theme* tokens as the body, so it re-skins with the
             host instead of drifting from it.

             Glyphs are Segoe MDL2 (E921 / E922 / E8BB), per the T3Lab standard
             for chrome buttons. Close hovers the Windows red.
             ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="CaptionButton" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Width"           Value="42"/>
            <Setter Property="Height"          Value="30"/>
            <Setter Property="Background"      Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground"      Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="bd" Background="{TemplateBinding Background}" CornerRadius="4">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeIconHoverBg}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="CaptionCloseButton" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource CaptionButton}">
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="bd" Background="{TemplateBinding Background}" CornerRadius="4">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="#E81123"/>
                                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="White"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ SIDE RAIL ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             40px rounded tiles in a 56px rail. The ACTIVE tile is filled with
             the Autodesk accent, not with near-black as the mock shows: on the
             dark theme Ink is near-white, so a "dark tile" would be a white tile
             holding a white icon. Accent is the one fill that reads on both.
             Icons are Path geometry — no glyph font to resolve, and they take
             the theme brush directly.
             ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Style x:Key="RailButton" TargetType="ToggleButton">
            <Setter Property="Width"            Value="40"/>
            <Setter Property="Height"           Value="40"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"           Value="0,0,0,6"/>
            <Setter Property="Background"       Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground"       Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeIconFg}"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="ToggleButton">
                        <Border x:Name="bd" Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                CornerRadius="4" SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeIconHoverBg}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeAccent}"/>
                                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeOnAccent}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

    </Window.Resources>

    <!-- ═══ DIALOG BODY ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         DockPanel with one 11px gutter, matching Revit's ChildWindow_Panel_Margin.
         No outer Border — the native window frame is the frame.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- The window frame is rounded to 8px because that is what Windows 11's DWM
         draws for every top-level window, Revit's dialogs included. Same principle
         as the 4px on buttons: the radius is the platform's, not ours. The Border
         clips to it so the content cannot square off the corners. -->
    <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource T3ThemePaneEdge}" BorderThickness="1"
            Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeDialogBg}"
            CornerRadius="8" ClipToBounds="True" SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="56"/>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="56"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <!-- ─── Left rail. Spans both rows so the app tile sits level with
                 the caption and the nav tiles continue straight below it. -->
            <Border Grid.Column="0" Grid.RowSpan="2"
                    Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeChromeBg}"
                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource T3ThemePaneEdge}" BorderThickness="0,0,1,0">
                <StackPanel Margin="0,8,0,0" WindowChrome.IsHitTestVisibleInChrome="True">

                    <!-- App tile: a card, not a button. It is identity, not an action. -->
                    <Border Width="40" Height="40" CornerRadius="4" Margin="0,0,0,14"
                            Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeChatBg}"
                            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource T3ThemeCardBorder}" BorderThickness="1"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center">
                        <Path Data="M 0 0 H 16 V 16 H 0 Z M 6 0 V 16 M 6 6 H 16"
                              Stroke="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}" StrokeThickness="1.4"
                              Width="17" Height="17" Stretch="Uniform"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    </Border>

                    <ToggleButton Style="{StaticResource RailButton}" IsChecked="True" ToolTip="Views">
                        <Path Data="M 2 5 L 9 1.5 L 16 5 L 9 8.5 Z M 2 9 L 9 12.5 L 16 9 M 2 12.5 L 9 16 L 16 12.5"
                              Stroke="{Binding Foreground, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ToggleButton}}"
                              StrokeThickness="1.4" StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                              Width="18" Height="18" Stretch="Uniform"/>
                    </ToggleButton>

                    <ToggleButton Style="{StaticResource RailButton}" ToolTip="Sheets">
                        <Path Data="M 2 1 H 12 L 16 5 V 17 H 2 Z M 12 1 V 5 H 16 M 5 9 H 13 M 5 12 H 13"
                              Stroke="{Binding Foreground, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ToggleButton}}"
                              StrokeThickness="1.4" StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                              Width="18" Height="18" Stretch="Uniform"/>
                    </ToggleButton>

                    <ToggleButton Style="{StaticResource RailButton}" ToolTip="Export">
                        <Path Data="M 1 9 H 14 M 10 5 L 14 9 L 10 13 M 16 1 V 17"
                              Stroke="{Binding Foreground, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ToggleButton}}"
                              StrokeThickness="1.4" StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                              Width="18" Height="18" Stretch="Uniform"/>
                    </ToggleButton>

                </StackPanel>
            </Border>

            <!-- ─── Caption. Title + subtitle on the left, window buttons right.
                 CaptionHeight on the WindowChrome makes the whole strip draggable;
                 the buttons opt back out with IsHitTestVisibleInChrome. -->
            <Grid Grid.Column="1" Grid.Row="0" Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeChromeBg}">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="14,0,0,0">
                    <TextBlock Text="UI Standard Showcase" FontSize="14" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                               Foreground="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"/>
                    <TextBlock Text="Revit-native reference for T3Lab tool windows"
                               FontSize="11" Margin="0,1,0,0"
                               Foreground="{DynamicResource T3ThemeAccent}"/>
                </StackPanel>

                <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            Margin="0,0,8,0" WindowChrome.IsHitTestVisibleInChrome="True">
                    <Button x:Name="btn_minimize" Style="{StaticResource CaptionButton}"
                            Click="minimize_button_clicked" ToolTip="Minimize">
                        <TextBlock Text="&#xE921;" FontFamily="Segoe MDL2 Assets" FontSize="10"/>
                    </Button>
                    <Button x:Name="btn_maximize" Style="{StaticResource CaptionButton}"
                            Click="maximize_button_clicked" ToolTip="Maximize">
                        <TextBlock Text="&#xE922;" FontFamily="Segoe MDL2 Assets" FontSize="10"/>
                    </Button>
                    <Button x:Name="btn_close" Style="{StaticResource CaptionCloseButton}"
                            Click="close_button_clicked" ToolTip="Close">
                        <TextBlock Text="&#xE8BB;" FontFamily="Segoe MDL2 Assets" FontSize="10"/>
                    </Button>
                </StackPanel>

                <Border Grid.ColumnSpan="2" Height="1" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                        Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemePaneEdge}"/>
            </Grid>

    <DockPanel Grid.Column="1" Grid.Row="1" Margin="7">

        <!-- ─── MainBottom: ONE status line, then the actions. Docked first so
             it owns the bottom edge, exactly as Revit's MainBottom does.

             The progress bar lives INSIDE this line, ~150px wide, and is hidden
             while idle. A full-width bar across the foot of a dialog is a
             dashboard pattern: it is the heaviest thing on screen and it is
             there even when nothing is running. Revit keeps its progress small
             and in the status strip, and so does this.

             The copyright takes the slot Revit gives its Help hyperlink — the
             least foreign home for it in this layout. A hairline above the row
             separates the footer from the content the way a Revit dialog does. -->
        <!-- No fill of its own. A Revit dialog separates its button row with a
             single rule, not with a tinted strip. -->
        <Border DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Margin="0,10,0,0" Padding="0,9,0,0"
                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource T3ThemeDivider}" BorderThickness="0,1,0,0">
            <Grid>
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <TextBlock Text="© Copyright by T3Lab" FontSize="11" Foreground="#F59E0B" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    <Border Width="1" Height="11" Margin="9,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeDivider}"/>
                    <ProgressBar x:Name="progress_bar" Style="{StaticResource DialogProgressBar}"
                                 Width="150" Value="75" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                 Margin="0,0,8,0"/>
                    <TextBlock x:Name="status_text" Text="Ready" FontSize="11"
                               Foreground="{DynamicResource T3ThemeMuted}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- Order is Revit's own, verified against its dialog footer:
                     OK · Cancel · Apply · Help. Apply starts disabled and enables
                     when something actually changed — that greyed-out Apply is
                     part of how a Revit dialog reads. Help is a real button, not
                     a hyperlink, in this footer. -->
                <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <Button Style="{StaticResource DialogDefaultButton}" Content="OK"     Click="close_button_clicked"/>
                    <Button Style="{StaticResource DialogButton}"        Content="Cancel" IsCancel="True" Click="close_button_clicked"/>
                    <Button Style="{StaticResource DialogButton}" x:Name="btn_apply" Content="Apply" IsEnabled="False"/>
                    <Button Style="{StaticResource DialogButton}" x:Name="btn_help"  Content="Help"/>
                </StackPanel>
            </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- ─── Options pane, left. Group boxes straight on the dialog face —
             where Revit puts its parameters. -->
        <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Left" Width="238" Margin="0,0,11,0">

            <GroupBox Style="{StaticResource DialogGroupBox}" Header="Scope">
                <StackPanel>
                    <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource FieldLabel}" Text="Rule set"/>
                    <ComboBox Style="{StaticResource DialogComboBox}" SelectedIndex="0" Margin="0,0,0,9">
                        <ComboBoxItem Content="Project standard 2026"/>
                        <ComboBoxItem Content="Strict naming preset"/>
                        <ComboBoxItem Content="Legacy 2024 mapping"/>
                    </ComboBox>

                    <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource FieldLabel}" Text="Name filter"/>
                    <TextBox Style="{StaticResource DialogTextBox}" Text="Plan_" Margin="0,0,0,9"/>

                    <RadioButton Style="{StaticResource DialogRadioButton}" Content="Entire project" IsChecked="True"/>
                    <RadioButton Style="{StaticResource DialogRadioButton}" Content="Current selection" Margin="0"/>
                </StackPanel>
            </GroupBox>

            <GroupBox Style="{StaticResource DialogGroupBox}" Header="Checks">
                <StackPanel>
                    <CheckBox Style="{StaticResource DialogCheckBox}" Content="View naming convention" IsChecked="True"/>
                    <CheckBox Style="{StaticResource DialogCheckBox}" Content="View template assigned" IsChecked="True"/>
                    <CheckBox Style="{StaticResource DialogCheckBox}" Content="Scope box alignment"/>
                    <CheckBox Style="{StaticResource DialogCheckBox}" Content="Sheet number sequence" IsEnabled="False" Margin="0"/>
                </StackPanel>
            </GroupBox>

            <!-- The theme preview lives here as an ordinary check box. It used to
                 be a glyph button in a custom caption bar — not a thing Revit has
                 anywhere, and the loudest redesign tell in the window. -->
            <GroupBox Style="{StaticResource DialogGroupBox}" Header="Preview" Margin="0">
                <CheckBox x:Name="chk_dark_preview" Style="{StaticResource DialogCheckBox}" Margin="0"
                          Content="Dark theme" Click="theme_preview_clicked"/>
            </GroupBox>

        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ─── List pane, fills the rest.
             The DataGrid is a DIRECT child of its Grid row — never wrapped in a
             ScrollViewer. A virtualising list inside a ScrollViewer is handed
             infinite height, so WPF realises every row at once; on a real model
             that is the freeze. It scrolls itself.

             Row virtualisation only. Column virtualisation is off because with
             four columns it buys nothing, not because it is broken — a collapsed
             Width="*" column seen while rendering this file off-screen turned
             out to be the one-pass layout in that harness, not this setting. -->
        <Grid>
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <!-- Filter row, laid out as Revit lays out its own: a labelled type
                 filter, then an unlabelled search field that fills the width. -->
            <Grid Grid.Row="0" Margin="0,0,0,5">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource MutedText}"
                           Text="Type" Margin="0,0,7,0"/>
                <ComboBox  Grid.Column="1" x:Name="type_filter" Style="{StaticResource DialogComboBox}"
                           Width="120" SelectedIndex="0" Margin="0,0,9,0">
                    <ComboBoxItem Content="All"/>
                    <ComboBoxItem Content="Floor Plan"/>
                    <ComboBoxItem Content="Elevation"/>
                    <ComboBoxItem Content="Section"/>
                    <ComboBoxItem Content="Schedule"/>
                </ComboBox>

                <Border Grid.Column="2" Style="{StaticResource SearchBoxChrome}" Margin="0,0,9,0">
                    <Grid>
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <Path Grid.Column="0" Margin="6,0,5,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              Data="M 4.6 0.8 A 3.8 3.8 0 1 0 4.6 8.4 A 3.8 3.8 0 1 0 4.6 0.8 M 7.5 7.5 L 10.6 10.6"
                              Stroke="{DynamicResource T3ThemeIconFg}" StrokeThickness="1.2"
                              StrokeStartLineCap="Round" StrokeEndLineCap="Round"/>
                        <Grid Grid.Column="1">
                            <TextBox x:Name="showcase_search_box"
                                     Background="Transparent" BorderThickness="0" Padding="0,0,4,0"
                                     Foreground="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"
                                     CaretBrush="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"
                                     VerticalContentAlignment="Center"/>
                            <TextBlock Text="Search name and other column values"
                                       Foreground="{DynamicResource T3ThemeFaint}"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" IsHitTestVisible="False">
                                <TextBlock.Style>
                                    <Style TargetType="TextBlock">
                                        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed"/>
                                        <Style.Triggers>
                                            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Text, ElementName=showcase_search_box}" Value="">
                                                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible"/>
                                            </DataTrigger>
                                        </Style.Triggers>
                                    </Style>
                                </TextBlock.Style>
                            </TextBlock>
                        </Grid>
                    </Grid>
                </Border>

                <TextBlock Grid.Column="3" x:Name="count_text" Style="{StaticResource MutedText}"
                           Text="10 views, 3 selected"/>
            </Grid>

            <DataGrid x:Name="sample_grid" Grid.Row="1"
                      AutoGenerateColumns="False" IsReadOnly="True"
                      HeadersVisibility="Column" RowHeaderWidth="0"
                      SelectionMode="Extended" SelectionUnit="FullRow"
                      CanUserSortColumns="True" CanUserResizeRows="False"
                      AlternationCount="2"
                      EnableRowVirtualization="True"
                      VirtualizingPanel.IsVirtualizing="True"
                      VirtualizingPanel.VirtualizationMode="Recycling"
                      ScrollViewer.CanContentScroll="True"
                      Background="{DynamicResource T3ThemeChatBg}"
                      Foreground="{DynamicResource T3ThemeInk}"
                      BorderBrush="{DynamicResource T3ThemeCardBorder}" BorderThickness="1"
                      GridLinesVisibility="Horizontal"
                      HorizontalGridLinesBrush="{DynamicResource T3ThemeGridLine}"
                      ColumnHeaderStyle="{StaticResource GridHeader}"
                      RowStyle="{StaticResource GridRow}"
                      CellStyle="{StaticResource GridCell}">
                <DataGrid.Resources>
                    <Style TargetType="ScrollBar" BasedOn="{StaticResource ThinScrollBar}"/>
                </DataGrid.Resources>
                <DataGrid.Columns>
                    <DataGridTextColumn Header="ID" Binding="{Binding id}" Width="72">
                        <DataGridTextColumn.ElementStyle>
                            <Style TargetType="TextBlock">
                                <Setter Property="Foreground"        Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeMuted}"/>
                                <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
                                <Style.Triggers>
                                    <DataTrigger Value="True"
                                                 Binding="{Binding IsSelected, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=DataGridRow}}">
                                        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeOnAccent}"/>
                                    </DataTrigger>
                                </Style.Triggers>
                            </Style>
                        </DataGridTextColumn.ElementStyle>
                    </DataGridTextColumn>
                    <DataGridTextColumn Header="Name"     Binding="{Binding name}"     Width="*"/>
                    <DataGridTextColumn Header="Category" Binding="{Binding category}" Width="110"/>
                    <!-- Status is plain text, coloured only when it is a problem —
                         the way Revit's own Manage Links and warning lists read.
                         A filled pill with a dot is the tell of a foreign table. -->
                    <DataGridTextColumn Header="Status" Binding="{Binding status}" Width="180">
                        <DataGridTextColumn.ElementStyle>
                            <Style TargetType="TextBlock">
                                <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
                                <Style.Triggers>
                                    <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding status}" Value="Non-Compliant (Template missing)">
                                        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeDanger}"/>
                                    </DataTrigger>
                                    <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding status}" Value="Non-Compliant (Naming standard)">
                                        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeDanger}"/>
                                    </DataTrigger>
                                    <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding status}" Value="Needs Review">
                                        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeWarning}"/>
                                    </DataTrigger>
                                    <!-- Last, so it wins: on a selected row the fill is the
                                         accent and a red or amber status would be unreadable
                                         on it. Selection outranks status. -->
                                    <DataTrigger Value="True"
                                                 Binding="{Binding IsSelected, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=DataGridRow}}">
                                        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource T3ThemeOnAccent}"/>
                                    </DataTrigger>
                                </Style.Triggers>
                            </Style>
                        </DataGridTextColumn.ElementStyle>
                    </DataGridTextColumn>
                </DataGrid.Columns>
            </DataGrid>

            <!-- Action strip under the list — the Manage Links / Manage Views
                 idiom. This replaces the icon toolbar the window used to carry;
                 Revit dialogs do not have toolbars. -->
            <StackPanel Grid.Row="2" Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,7,0,0">
